1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,339
The brush clears a wooden ship, with a square sail and a high stern.

2
00:00:05,339 --> 00:00:11,963
Ships like this carried pepper, pearls and cloth across the sea to Rome. They came back with gold.

3
00:00:11,963 --> 00:00:14,963
How did pepper pay for Roman gold?

4
00:00:33,317 --> 00:00:41,605
How do we know? The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea, written by a Greek sailor about the first century CE, is a guide to ports.

5
00:00:41,605 --> 00:00:48,913
Archaeologists dug up Arikamedu. And Roman gold and silver coins are found in hoards in the south.

6
00:01:05,783 --> 00:01:13,923
Trade between India and the Roman world grew from about the first century BCE. It was by sea, helped by the monsoon winds.

7
00:01:13,923 --> 00:01:20,949
A Greek sailor named Hippalus is said to have discovered the pattern of the winds. The Periplus lists the ports.

8
00:01:20,949 --> 00:01:30,822
Muziris and Tondi were on the Malabar coast. Arikamedu was on the east coast. Barygaza, now Bharuch, was on the west coast.

9
00:01:30,822 --> 00:01:37,859
India sent spices, mainly pepper, along with pearls, ivory, cotton and fine cloth, and gems.

10
00:01:37,859 --> 00:01:43,980
Rome sent gold and silver coins, wine in jars called amphorae, glass and coral.

11
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:50,898
Pliny, a Roman writer, complained that India drained Rome of gold, and gave a very large figure.

12
00:01:50,898 --> 00:01:54,288
Many traders from the Roman world settled on the coast.

13
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:59,866
The trade also enriched Indian guilds, and the Satavahana and Tamil kingdoms.

14
00:02:16,717 --> 00:02:25,323
Old name, new name. The Periplus calls the western port Barygaza. Today the city is called Bharuch, in Gujarat.

15
00:02:25,323 --> 00:02:30,098
It stands on the Narmada, and has been a port for over two thousand years.

16
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:53,422
Carve this tip into memory. Periplus, pepper, monsoon. Roman gold.

17
00:02:53,422 --> 00:02:59,528
Four words to cover the Indo-Roman trade: a book, a spice, a wind and a coin.

18
00:03:16,383 --> 00:03:32,851
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which book is a Greek sailor’s guide to the ports? The answer: the Periplus. Number two.

19
00:03:32,851 --> 00:03:44,574
Which foreign coins reached South India? The answer: Roman gold and silver coins. Number three.

20
00:03:44,574 --> 00:03:55,842
Which Indian spice was the main export? The answer: pepper. Now one line for Mains.

21
00:03:55,842 --> 00:03:59,978
Examine the nature and impact of Indo-Roman trade.

22
00:04:20,850 --> 00:04:30,788
The ship goes into its case. It carried pepper and pearls across the sea. Three facts to take away. A ship that rode the monsoon.

23
00:04:30,788 --> 00:04:40,940
Trade with Rome, about 100 BCE to 300 CE. Pepper out, gold in. Next gallery: The Rise of the Guptas.

